-- title:
--   Cuspidal vectors meet N-induced span trivially and are τ-fixed
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $K$ a field of characteristic zero, $V$ a $K$-vector space and $\rho$ a representation of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ on $V$ over $K$; for $t \in \mathbb{Z}/q$ write $u_t$ for the element of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ given by the matrix $\begin{pmatrix}1&t\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ (with inverse $\begin{pmatrix}1&-t\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$), as defined by [`CuspidalType.unipotent`](def/CuspidalType_IsCuspidalOfType.html#L27). Let $C$ denote the intersection, over all $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$, of the kernels of the $K$-linear maps $\bigl(\sum_{t \in \mathbb{Z}/q} \rho(u_t)\bigr) \circ \rho(g)$. Assume given a $K$-linear endomorphism $\tau$ of $V$ with $\tau \circ \rho(g) = \rho(g) \circ \tau$ for every $g$, and a $K$-subspace $T \le V$ contained in the $K$-span of the set of all vectors of the form $\rho(g)v$ with $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ and $v \in V$ satisfying $\rho(u_t)v = v$ for all $t \in \mathbb{Z}/q$, and assume $\tau v - v \in T$ for every $v \in V$. The conclusion is twofold: $C \cap T = 0$, and $\tau v = v$ for every $v \in C$. No finiteness hypothesis on $\dim_K V$ is imposed.
--
--   The subspace $C$ is the cuspidal part of $V$ for the upper unipotent subgroup $N$ of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/q)$, and the span occurring in the hypothesis is the part of $V$ generated by translates of $N$-fixed vectors; the statement says that these two are disjoint and that any equivariant operator differing from the identity only inside the second acts trivially on the first. It is used in the vanishing criteria for cuspidal specialisations on the full-level modular curve, in the form of the three declarations [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.eq_zero_of_cuspidalSpecialization_eq_zero_of_forall_sum_unipotent_eq_zero_of_semistableCovering_of_reduction`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.eq_zero_of_cuspidalSpecialization_eq_zero_of_forall_sum_unipotent_eq_zero_of_semistableCovering_of_reduction) and its variants for the residue characteristics $2$ and $3$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspidalType_iInf_ker_sum_unipotent_comp_inf_eq_bot_and_apply_eq_self_of_le_span_unipotent_fixed_of_sub_mem.lean

